k us off  on that one i guess that is a very  annoying answer i'm going to come up  with i think you have to start by  defining artistic merit and i think you  have to start by saying  who is the person who judges what  artistic merit is  so even before we get on to these  aspects i think you have to look at who  is it who's actually  setting the critical agenda today um it  used to be our critics  uh  there's a big temptation to say now that  it's perhaps gallerists like gagosian  you know who spread their  tentacles across the world with  galleries and can show artists  right across the world and compromi  promote them in ways that was probably  not possible before  so i guess that um  my question really and i think we should  be discussing is is  how today do we judge artistic merit and  once  if we can possibly work that one out  then we can see whether money  fame and status  correspond to that so that would be my  rather annoying first comment opening  gambit thank you georgina uh janna  is art now more about money fame and  status than artistic merit your three  minutes starts now when we talk about  the art market and the art scene  my answer would be yes absolutely money  is money and fame and status is  unfortunately determining where the  attention goes  but if we then look at from the artist's  point of view  then money famous status does not  necessarily play in at all it really  depends on the individual artist  what you choose if you simply just need  to express something then you you don't  go for the status of money and there are  other types of artists where you know  the status or the money comes absolutely  first  and unfortunately then they also get the  attention because that's the world we  live in right now  at least in the short term not  necessarily in the longer term  but  you know on the  third level what i also want to do is  distinguish between art forms because  um  speculation the kind of money  speculation we see when it is you know 