rtist calls himself people it's  mike winkleman  and the only thing that makes this so  valuable it's it's 5 000 different posts  he's done online since 2007 that he's  put together in one but is that the  person who buys it gets like an  authenticated line that says he  owns  this one singular piece but as i  understand it's because of it being the  internet a lot of people can actually  copy it and and then  it will exist for others but he will  have the authorized version that you  know is the 69 million worth so i think  we live in a money world that is is  almost like an illusion except  it's there it's for real in a strange  way and influences are so yeah this was  my  attempt to have  separated into some different levels  thank you so much and patty over to you  you know i think georgina really got at  the heart of it when she talked about um  value and what how value is arrived at  and i think what we're talking about  often when we talk about value um and  cultural value is how much attention  something gets right and how we get  attention to that particular thing  because ultimately i think if we look at  what art the function of art just sort  of generally in like the most kind of  pie in the sky version of what we think  art is it's an expression and reflection  of cultural values but it's also what we  want the world to be so placing a value  on that um is  through an attention economy is also  like reflecting our own values  now how those values used to come used  to be determined was through a small  handful of white men right we had  clement greenberg um we had michael  freeman we had these white critics and  they yielded a lot awful lot of power  and what we ended up with was a cannon  that was very white and filled with  white men right so we knew that was that  was the  a flawed way of going about it so what  is different with the world that we have  now in instagram  um and twitter and like this diverse  audience  well  it's still controlled by a